Linux Process & Service Management — Simple Notes

1. What is a Process?

Every time you run a program (nginx, java, python, etc.), Linux starts a process for it. Each process gets a unique number called a PID (Process ID).

2. Viewing / Checking Processes

ps -ef
Lists all running processes.

ps aux
Lists all running processes (different format, very common).

ps aux | grep java
Filters the list to show only lines containing "java".

ps aux | grep java | grep -v grep
Same as above, but removes the grep command itself from the results, so you only see the real process, not your search command.

Why the extra grep -v grep?
When you run ps aux | grep java, the search command itself shows up as a line in the output, because it also contains the word "java". Adding grep -v grep hides that fake match, so if nothing real is running, you get zero results.

3. Killing a Process

Why kill a process?
Sometimes an application, like a Java app, hangs or freezes and uses too much CPU or memory, starving other processes. You may get a Jira ticket asking you to find and kill such a process, or you may need to do it during planned downtime.

How to do it:
First, view or check the process to find its PID using ps -ef or ps aux.
Then kill it using the PID.

kill (PID)
Normal, graceful kill request. Example: kill 4055

kill -9 (PID)
Force kill. Example: kill -9 4040. This is the classic interview question: how do you forcefully kill a process?

kill -3 (PID)
Doesn't kill the process, it dumps the thread logs. Useful when a developer says the app is using too much memory and asks you to share the thread dump or logs.

kill -STOP (PID)
Pauses the process temporarily, doesn't kill it.

kill -CONT (PID)
Resumes a paused process.

4. Services (Background Processes)

A service is a background process that starts automatically when the server boots up, and restarts automatically if the server restarts.

Simple analogy: when your laptop restarts, an app you had set to auto-start comes back up on its own. That's exactly how a service behaves.

Process vs service, which is which?
Something like a Python or Java app you start manually is a process.
A web server, like Apache or Nginx, is usually set up as a service, so if the Linux server goes down and comes back up, the web server automatically comes back online too.

You can convert a process into a service, but common tools like Apache and Nginx already come with this built in.

Managing services:

systemctl list-units --type=service
Lists all services running on the machine.

systemctl stop cron.service
Stops a specific service, example given is cron.

systemctl start (service)
Starts a service.

systemctl restart (service)
Restarts a service.

5. Monitoring CPU, Memory and Disk

Common questions devops engineers get asked:
Which process is using the most memory?
Which process is using the most CPU?
Which process is using the most disk?

ps aux lists everything, but checking each line manually is painful. Better tools below.

top
Real-time, live view of processes, shows PID, CPU percent, memory percent, updates every second. Highest resource-consuming processes appear at the top.

htop
Same info as top, but with a nicer, more visual interface.

vmstat
Reports overall system performance. Useful when debugging an instance to see if it's under memory pressure.

free -m
Shows memory used and free, static snapshot, in MB.

free -h
Same as above, but in human-readable units like GB or MB.

nproc
Shows how many CPUs the machine has.

df -h
Shows disk space usage per partition, human-readable.

Note: top and htop are visual tools, great for a human watching the screen, but not usable inside automation scripts. For scripts, use free -m, ps aux, etc, which give plain text output you can filter with grep.

In real-world setups, these commands are often complemented by monitoring tools like Prometheus and Grafana for dashboards and alerts.

6. Disk Management Key Commands

df -h
Tells you if the whole machine is running out of disk space. Shows all partitions, the / filesystem is the important one.

du -sh folder/
Tells you what inside one specific folder is taking up space.

ps aux
A snapshot of what's currently running.

top
A live, refreshing view of what's running.

percent CPU inside top
How hard each individual process is working the processor.

Quick Practice Checklist

Try these in order on a test Linux machine:

1. ps aux, see everything running
2. ps aux | grep (name), search for a specific app
3. ps aux | grep (name) | grep -v grep, clean search, removes the grep noise
4. top, watch live CPU and memory usage
5. kill (PID) then kill -9 (PID), practice graceful vs forced kill
6. kill -STOP (PID) then kill -CONT (PID), practice pause and resume
7. systemctl list-units --type=service, see running services
8. free -h and df -h, check memory and disk space
